<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Pascoe Fawkner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Pascoe Fawkner was an Australian pioneer, businessman and politician who helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> in 1835. Raised in Van Diemen's Land, he sent a party on the schooner Enterprize to the Yarra River in 1835 and soon settled there himself. He opened Melbourne's first hotel and published its first newspaper, and later served in Victoria's Legislative Council.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-pascoe-fawkner.jpg" alt="John Pascoe Fawkner" width="320" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patterson Bros. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Pascoe_Fawkner_1862_SLNSW_FL3281745.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fawkner was born near Cripplegate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 20 October 1792, the son of John Fawkner, a metal refiner, and Hannah Pascoe. When his father was sentenced to fourteen years for receiving stolen goods, the family sailed with him on HMS Calcutta to found a British settlement in Bass Strait, landing at Sullivan Bay, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a>, in 1803. Lieutenant-Governor David Collins abandoned the site in 1804 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a>, where the young Fawkner worked on the family farm, in a saw-pit and in his father's bakery.</p><p>In 1814 he helped seven convicts build a lugger at Recherche Bay for an escape to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>; when the vessel was taken, he was sentenced to 500 lashes and hard labour. In December 1819 he moved to Launceston with Eliza Cobb, a transported convict, whom he married on 5 December 1822. There they ran a bakery, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> business and a bookshop, from 1826 the Cornwall Hotel, and from 1829 the newspaper The Launceston Advertiser.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Frustrated with the government of Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-arthur">George Arthur</a>, Fawkner bought the topsail schooner Enterprize in April 1835. Creditors kept him from sailing in August, but the ship entered the Yarra River on 15 August 1835 and moored at the foot of present-day William Street, where his party landed on 30 August; Fawkner himself arrived on 16 October. He published the district's first newspaper, the Melbourne Advertiser, from 1 January 1838, its first editions written by hand, and from 1839 the Port Phillip Patriot.</p><p>He was elected one of the Market Commissioners in 1842 and a town councillor in 1843, sat in the first Victorian Legislative Council from 1851 until it was abolished in 1856, and from November 1856 represented Central Province in the first Parliament of self-governing Victoria until his death. He argued that all sane men above the age of twenty-one should vote and that no property should be required for office, and he strongly opposed the transportation of convicts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fawkner made many enemies but died as the grand old man of the colony, in Collingwood on 4 September 1869, at the age of 77. Some 15,000 people were reported to have lined the streets for his public funeral. The suburbs of Fawkner and Pascoe Vale and Fawkner Park are named after him, and a replica of the Enterprize was launched in 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Pascoe Fawkner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlers sent by <b>John Pascoe Fawkner</b> went ashore on the Yarra River, building the first houses of what became Melbourne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers from Van Diemen's Land, led by John Batman and <b>John Pascoe Fawkner</b>, founded Melbourne in 1835 on Kulin land, and the area was governed as the Port Phillip District of the Colony of New South Wales.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Colony of Victoria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1842" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Pascoe Fawkner"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1842">What was here in 1842? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
